{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow","id":1492896,"title":"How fast do you think Vampires should grow?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-09-12T10:53:52-07:00","id":1492896,"title":"How fast do you think Vampires should grow?","content":"While xp costs might inform our decisions (First edition Vampire offering markedly lower costs for disciplines than latter editions) the rate which we get XP varies considerably and... is largely a convenience for game purposes; It's very likely that the storyteller will have a coterie where not everyone will put the same effort in each night: Character A spends much of his downtime reading on a vast array of subjects and occasionally experiments, Character B Trains her ass off physically with expert instructors. Character C administers his territories and holdings, and character D... bums around. Let's assume D is the worst-case scenario and spends their time uncritically watching the most mind-numbing entertainments and drinks blood out of bags, not even hunting or rekindling human connections. <br /> <br /> Mechanically, they're all equal in most games, and that's probably best for the players.... Well, not entirely. Vampire A could make more gains if he was learning rituals, C has a definite advantage since Backgrounds don't cost XP and downtime is a great time to get them. B, unfortunately, is wasting effort, since they're not getting anything more than D and most given downtime XP rates are really too low for high-intensity training. And that feels a little weird to me. Vampire bodies don't fatigue, they usually have resources and thus access to elite trainers. After hunting, they could easily spend 4+ hours a night getting really good at a lot of things. <br /> <br /> Perhaps the Beast will push Vampire D to do more. Such idle behaviour aggravates the Beast or something. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And then we get into <i>how </i>vampires gain abilities. They're not Humans. Their minds can learn but I'm not sure their muscles would gain anything no matter how much you bench or squat. And how does one gain intelligence? Don't tell me brain training is real and that it can take you from 1 dot to 5 with enough practice (I mean I don't actually know but from what I can see it just sounds like a great way to sell products, please feel free to share science) <br /> <br /> I'm of the opinion that vampires gain Disciplines from the methods used to hunt, Attributes from hunting people with advantageous qualities, and abilities from... ordinary training, though perhaps the blood of people with that skill help things along. Virtues and willpower can come from character building moments. I suspect vampires will be a little slower than normal humans at acquiring skills, but in turn they don't forget the skills they've learnt, allowing them to accumulate considerable acumen over the centuries without constantly having to practice skill retention. <br /> <br /> I had the idea that maybe instead of around 3xp a session to be spent in any way the player choses, they would have 1xp for disciplines, 1 xp for attributes, and 1 xp for skills. The first two are the natural results of feeding. The latter obviously in practicing skills. <br /> <br /> <i>A similar thought for werewolf: Garou probably actually practice and reinforce skills when they acquire Gifts, since Gifts are often accompanied by a challenge which tests the character. Ergo it's a little strange to sacrifice XP that could be used for skills in order to obtain gifts. Ditto Infernalists. </i><br /> <br /> <br /> However, early nights are really very important. I kinda expect fledglings and early-stage neonates to be training their asses off and getting like.... 30 xp a month or something. Thoughts? <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T12:44:48-07:00","id":1492911,"title":"","content":"Vampiric growth is one of those things makes less and less sense the more you think about it. It routinely breaks the setting depending on what you are prioritizing in the simulations.<br /> <br /> <b>Game XP:</b> <br /> At the end of every game, the PCs get some XP, but this breaks down due to the fact that not everyone has the same session frequency. If we assume a baseline of 5 XP per session to keep the math simple, that means a group that plays once a week and a group that plays daily or just several times a week will have completely different progression speeds. This is just a game mechanic that sits apart from the setting or the fluff.<br /> <br /> <b>Vampires are the same:</b> <br /> There is no clan or bloodline that has a bonus to XP gains, the closest you get is In-Clan Discipline cost reductions. The True Brujah have a clan weakness issue for paying more XP for increasing specific stats, but all the others don't have any such restriction. This would mean that all of vampire society should be advancing at the same rate. Thus, as fast as the PCs &quot;get good&quot;, the antagonists should have an equal number of new tricks. Meaning that being active and older is practically a guaranteed win condition. So the best strategy would be to isolate yourself from all vampire society and keep self training, but that breaks the whole social monster thing that is at the core of VtM. I guess this is the reason why so many anime with RPG elements all have XP Boosters as one of the hero traits that set them apart.<br /> <br /> <b>Gaining Access by Doing:</b> <br /> As an IRL Martial Arts enthusiast I can easily agree with the sentiment that training gets results, but at the same time the stakes vampires play for are way higher than what most humans deal with. For every vampire that doesn't have Fortitude as a clan discipline, does that mean they have to constantly endure aggravated damage to learn it and then level it? How many vampires end up in the final death trying to gain a dot? Normally you see this potential downside when practicing magic but when you try to think of a training setup for the various disciplines there is a huge risk of collateral damage. Alternatively if you say that you have to learn on the job, rather than in training, that means if the ST decides to run the wrong kind of game, you can not advance. Are you a combat specialist, well the next 8 to 12 play sessions are nothing but politics and social manipulation. Are you a Necromancer, well no one has died in the area who was strong enough to become a ghost.<br /> <br /> <b>XP Costs as Game Design:</b> <br /> This is one of those things that doesn't get talked about much, but there is remarkably little balance in build costs. Back in Exalted 2nd edition there was a lot of discussion of exactly how many XP it took to create a combat character with very specific charms to ensure damage output and defense, off the top of my head for a Solar combat build it was something around 75 XP post character generation with you spending bonus points to take what you can and use the left over for character extras. Conversely if you wanted the same Solar to be a Crafter, you would have to spend all your bonus points on crafting picks, no room for customization, and then you couldn't functionally do your job as a crafter until 250 XP post character generation. Imagine a fighter no able to hit anything until 250 points into the game.<br /> <br /> VtM doesn't have as an extreme an XP build balance problem, but there are definitely some build types that largely XP sinks. A City Gangrel at 50 XP post character generation is far more functional than a Tremere Thaumaturgy specialist at the same XP level. This is then exacerbated by the fact that certain builds require fewer secondary stats than others. A sniper only needs Dexterity+Firearms with some Stealth dots, while a Business CEO type needs all the social stats and attributes on top of the stats required for his business.<br /> <br /> <b>Games don't Last:</b> <br /> The sad truth of most RPGs is that groups break up, people get bored, and life happens. As a result, most characters never get within sight of finishing a build, let alone completing the build and beginning to branch out. So, there is a real argument to be had about burning bonus points to get higher powers rather than higher stats. For example, if you put one dot in each discipline, to max out all three it will cost 150 XP post character generation. On the other hand, if you put all three points into one discipline, burn 14 bonus points to max out that discipline, then it will only cost you 120 XP to learn and max out the other two, with the downside that your other stats will be capped at 3 dots. Thus, you get more upfront benefit for the limited time the game runs.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>The Answer:</b> <br /> There isn't a one size fits all answer. Scale the XP up as the number of sessions per week goes down, keep in mind the differences in build costs to be fully functional and try to create scenarios that balance that so you don't appear to be showing favoritism, then come up with reasons why the other vampires either are not learning at the same rate or are incentivized to not spend their banked XP on abilities to maintain the power gap between them and the PCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T12:54:48-07:00","id":1492914,"title":"","content":"I think vampires will act mostly like real people. Very few people &quot;train their asses off&quot; at any point in time. Sure, a very small select group does - usually people in competitive sports, elite special forces soldiers who need to master certain skills to do their job, or certain scientists, engineers, and so on who need to quickly master some discipline as a result of some emergency. But all those people do so in an environment where there is already a large institutional framework to provide them with instructors, accumulated knowledge, equipment, and lots of resources so that those people can make &quot;learning&quot; their full time job for an extended period of time. Vampires actually don't have any of those things. The Tremere come closest, but your local chantry is basically just a special library with a mentor (who may have more important things to do than spend a lot of time on intense instruction of the newbie).<br /> <br /> The time of accelerated learning for vampires has already happened prior to when PCs start their character. The game assumes the average PC has had 5-25 years as a vampire before the first game session happens. That is when they were taught important skills by their sire like how to hunt, preserve the Masquerade, master their initial number of Disciplines, how to handle the Beast, and so on.<br /> <br /> If anything, I feel the normal process of granting XP in typical chronicles results in the PCs increasing their power far too much, too quickly. In a short amount of time, neonates can become very powerful even to many NPCs who are meant to be powerful. I don't mind PCs becoming more powerful than the Prince or a Primogen or even other elders. But I do have an issue with them doing it in the timespan of perhaps 1-2 years in game time. So I absolutely disagree with the idea that STs should be giving out 30 XP a month to the PCs.<br /> <br /> That's why I like to run historical games that can provide the illusion of time to reasonably accommodate all the XP they are getting. I generally go for a 20-50 period before the expected end of the chronicle. After a &quot;story&quot; that might run a few game sessions where PCs might spend a really short of time doing whatever, I'll then have some amount of downtime - maybe weeks or months, or in some special cases years - and start the next game session that much time after we left the last game. Of course, the more time spent in downtime, we as a gaming group will spend a large part of that game session confirming what has happened, what did they do, what did NPCs do, etc. so any of their long term plans can proceed or even come to fruition.<br /> <br /> I tend to view any population in a WoD game to be in one of three groups - the chumps, the lieutenants, and the movers &amp; shakers. Chumps advance slowly on the XP chart. Lieutenants sprint ahead, but not too much. Just enough to distinguish them as the leaders of the chumps. And the movers &amp; shakers gain a lot of XP relatively quickly. It's about a 60%/30%/10% division. I do &quot;level up&quot; NPCs over time, but most will fall behind the pace of the PCs.<br /> <br /> Of course, the PCs are always in the movers &amp; shakers category. Given enough time, the PCs will bypass the chumps and become equals to the lieutenants. By the end of the chronicle, they'll be able to become members of the mover &amp; shakers, but typically at the low end. If the chronicle is in a small city, they might be able to squeeze the existing movers &amp; shakers out. If they are in a large city that has attracted some very old and powerful elders, then there may be Kindred they need to be careful around, but they are still part of the local power structure. I think most games are like that - I just prefer a narrative it took them 50-100 years to do so rather than 3 (even though it's the same amount of game sessions).<br /> <br /> In real life, unique people like Alexander the Great and Napoleon can achieve immense success in a very short period of time. The same can be the same for entrepreneurs in brand new industries - Steve Jobs, Bill Gates, Elon Musk, and so on. But what happens to Napoleon if Frederick the Great, Julius Caesar, and Genghis Khan are still around? What happens to Zuckerberg if Bill Gates, the king of shady business practices, is still actively controlling his empire and has powers to do all sorts of illegal things to ruin the competition? That is the kind of environment vampires deal in. It doesn't mean these incredible individuals still won't move up and build their empires, it'll just be at a much slower pace.<br /> <br /> That's how I see it. But I know I ST my chronicles differently from most people. It's not how I use to run my chronicles, but my current method was born out of some dissatisfaction at the final results. I started changing things because a friend once ran a chronicle that started in the 1970s. He really liked that mid-late seventies feel degenerated decline of the era of disco, punk rock, Studio 54, black outs, famous serial killers, etc. I really liked how the actual passage of time impacted the feel of the game as we moved through the seventies into the early eighties. Our character progress seemed more satisfying as we tied it to actual events (both fictional and historical) in game, instead of it always being &quot;now&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now players do want to achieve certain XP milestones. They typically want at least one Disciple to reach level 5. They all want to learn more Disciplines. They want to achieve certain Background goals in game. So players may want to see more XP collectively at certain stages than I am giving out. If so, I might either adjust how much XP they getting per session, and/or award bonus XP during those downtimes. The key is aligning what the players want with what the ST needs, and the chronicle's setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T13:08:44-07:00","id":1492917,"title":"","content":"Realistically speaking, it is just going to vary. Just like in real life some people are going to be naturals who take to mastering things quickly and some people are going to be that guy who couldn't carry a tune with a bucket no matter how much effort they put into learning to sing. It wouldn't at all be like the game we play where everyone advances at the exact same rate by putting X amount of XP into getting a guaranteed Y amount of improvement.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Exalted Whose Name is Carved in Leaves of Jade<br /> Senator of the Greater Chamber<br /> Champion of the Silver Pact<br /> Chosen of the Little Gods<br /> Agent of Fate","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/988-mizu","name":"Mizu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T13:30:17-07:00","id":1492922,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow?p=1492914#post1492914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think vampires will act mostly like real people. Very few people &quot;train their asses off&quot; at any point in time. Sure, a very small select group does - usually people in competitive sports, elite special forces soldiers who need to master certain skills to do their job, or certain scientists, engineers, and so on who need to quickly master some discipline as a result of some emergency. But all those people do so in an environment where there is already a large institutional framework to provide them with instructors, accumulated knowledge, equipment, and lots of resources so that those people can make &quot;learning&quot; their full time job for an extended period of time. Vampires actually don't have any of those things. The Tremere come closest, but your local chantry is basically just a special library with a mentor (who may have more important things to do than spend a lot of time on intense instruction of the newbie).</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see where you are coming from, but I feel this approach ignores a couple of key concepts regarding vampire mechanics.<br /> <br /> Unlike normal offspring where you reproduce and have to wait around to see if they are worthwhile or what traits they pickup, a vampire can just choose the fully grown human with the best traits to embrace. Thus the few elites we have in society, the special forces, the top engineers, etc, will be chosen over those that don't have those traits or skills, thus skewing the behavior pattern of kindred society as a whole.<br /> <br /> Furthermore Kindred society and unlife in general isn't known for being forgiving or particularly kind to failure. Thus those mortals who lacked the elite traits or mental discipline will get killed going up against those supernaturals that do have them. A Brujah may embrace a gang member on a whim, a Nosferatu might embrace someone who is too pretty and arrogant, but both of them will lose going up against an Assamite who was a special forces sniper in their mortal life.<br /> <br /> This becomes even more of a stark contrast if you play power broker Background heavy games verses more street level lost boy style games. The coterie of vampire bikers can trash swaths of a town in a single night, but a coterie of Bankers and CEOs can cripple cities, states, or nations for decades from anywhere on the globe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T17:49:26-07:00","id":1492941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow?p=1492922#post1492922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unlike normal offspring where you reproduce and have to wait around to see if they are worthwhile or what traits they pickup, a vampire can just choose the fully grown human with the best traits to embrace. Thus the few elites we have in society, the special forces, the top engineers, etc, will be chosen over those that don't have those traits or skills, thus skewing the behavior pattern of kindred society as a whole.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think we have any reason to believe this is what is happening in the setting.<br /> <br /> First, most canonical characters don't have any of these kinds of elite background. Instead, we get Dickie Fulcher, Gengis, the Walenski twins, Sharon &amp; Michael Payne, Garwood Marshall, and Ben &amp; Paula Smith just to give some examples from Chicago By Night. Even someone powerful like Anabelle is mostly because of her potent blood and long life, not because she's some super elite person who spends all her time in &quot;training&quot;. Now we also have examples of characters that do fit that definition of elite. Nicolai showed magical promise early in life. Ballard and Capone would fit. There are plenty of other examples. But we also have an example of Lodin who pretty much tried to use your criteria when creating his second brood... and his results were mixed. He had some winners like Kevin Jackson and the Hurricane, but also losers like Peterson and Schumpeter.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, embracing really effective people might backfire. The people you embrace might become too ambitious and seek to overthrow you. This was a big problem that Lodin had - his rather large and successful brood was brimming with resent towards him. Worse, what if the person who you embrace is like Nathaniel, who so hates his condition and sire he actively tries to destroy you and the rest of his kind? Or that he commits suicide? There are so many other criteria that must be considered besides how incredibly effective the target may be as a vampire. The embrace is not the same as giving them some Potion of Immortal Life.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, you forget that in the setting most vampires need permission to embrace. You may only get one chance to create a childe ever, or wait a very long time for the next one. Why choose someone whose only merit is how much better she might be than you? Why not because you desire a companion, you become infatuated with them, to &quot;save&quot; someone from an early death, or for some other reason?<br /> <br /> I have no problem with these kind of concepts as vampires. But they certainly aren't the only concepts in the setting. Instead, the canonical characters are usually a mix of chumps, lieutenants, and movers &amp; shakers. And what role they had as a mortal is not indicative of what they might become as vampires.<br /> <br /> And I think that is a good thing. It would be a boring game if every NPC was some kind of special forces soldier, brilliant scientist, amazing business person, etc. Furthermore, wouldn't it suck for the playerss if all their NPC opponents were these kind of super-elite rivals? Who wants to be fighting some combination of Batman/Captain America/Steve Jobs/Napoleon all the time? I'd rather be crossing swords with a range of opponents from morons like Dickie Fulcher to competent foes like Kevin Jackson.<br /> <br /> Now we know many players will gravitate to this kind of thing because they want their PCs to be special and fantastic. That's fine. I tend to roll my eyes when the players aren't looking when I encounter YET ANOTHER Brujah/Gangrel Navy Seal, financial wizard Ventrue, Toreador martial artist or unrecognized genius, etc. I'd rather see more rounded concepts that don't fall into the category of &quot;PC tropes&quot;. But no matter how they define their character concept, everyone knows the PCs are supposed to be special and will advance quickly.... simply because they are the PCs. It doesn't matter if they are the ex-Navy Seal, or some janitor dying of cancer that a Nosferatu liked. As PCs, both the janitor and Navy Seal have the same build points and be gaining more or less the same XP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T19:10:54-07:00","id":1492944,"title":"","content":"First off I think that the XP perspective is completely failed from the onset. XP is one of the most contrived abstractions in RPGs ever, despite being a staple and something I'm mostly happy with. No XP system conveys realistic growth for any kind of character, most not even pretend to, and WoD isn't an exception on either. PCs get XP because they're mandated protagonists at the hands of players that need a set rule for expected growth, and that's it.<br /> <br /> Beyond that there are many facets to this question. I usually don't like to be too strict in defining how mystic processes happen in the game, as long as they don't hinder the game. Growing Attributes, specially physical ones, is certainly a mystic process for a <i>dead body</i>, so who knows? One vampire gains them through consumption, another for some reason does seem to get real benefit from exercising even if by all accounts that shouldn't work. Maybe they're channeling belief, or blood into this? Maybe just having blood in your system twists your body over time and enhance (or damage!) your attributes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow?p=1492922#post1492922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Furthermore Kindred society and unlife in general isn't known for being forgiving or particularly kind to failure.</div> </div> </div> </div>Adding to what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> said, I think this miss something of the big picture of living in a society, even if it is a supernatural one:<br /> <br /> Mediocrity is the norm.<br /> <br /> Successful people aren't mediocre most of the time, or at least when it really matters. But success is something that exists in contrast to those who didn't get it. And when we talk society, there's more nuance to it than just failure.<br /> <br /> Behind the successful entrepreneur there are droves of salary workers. They're not failures. They get what they need to feed their families and lives. Likewise, a Prince is only successful if they have a city to command, with lots of vampires that aren't Princes nor will ever be. Yes, some will succumb at some point, but if people are dying all the time the Prince isn't really successful. Behind their success is a number of vampires with stable lives that can't hold a candle to the movers and shakers of Kindred society, but they manage to fulfill their needs. They may be still incredibly powerful compared to mortals, but among the Kindred they're mediocre. Even if just in context.<br /> <br /> Unless your version of the WoD is a constant battle royal where only the fittest manage as much as to survive a year, most vampires will do what everyone does, they'll find their comfort zone and stay there. They'll pick the fights they know they'll win and avoid uncertainty, they'll leave trouble for those who want to deal with it, and they'll follow the only really tested and true method of survival against things bigger than them: lay down, don't draw attention and let them eat someone stupider than you. Make yourself useful enough to not be a liability and you're gold, that's more than enough.<br /> <br /> Those people aren't PCs or important NPCs, of course. But they're the social background of the story, and actually the only real definition of what a &quot;typical&quot; or &quot;average&quot; vampire should be like, not the important characters. Those are special by definition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow#post1492896\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And how does one gain intelligence? Don't tell me brain training is real and that it can take you from 1 dot to 5 with enough practice (I mean I don't actually know but from what I can see it just sounds like a great way to sell products, please feel free to share science)</div> </div> </div> </div>Since you asked:<br /> <br /> &quot;Intelligence&quot; is a tricky definition to begin with, and while it is the longest studied mental faculty, the very first thing a Psychologist ever studied in this world, the question if we can get smarter by training still raises a huge variety of answers among specialists.<br /> <br /> But the actual hard part is to define it, and technical definitions of Intelligence are like every technical definition of something: a language tool for specialists to discuss, that has loads of meaning to them, but has little to do with colloquial usage.<br /> <br /> All in all, I'd say the best answer to this question without going too much into details is <b>yes</b><b>, we can improve intelligence, and do so with training</b>. We can improve many things that are aspects of Intelligence or somehow affect it, and we can improve many skill that aren't <i>technically</i> Intelligence, but for practical purposes in our daily lives are just as good.<br /> <br /> To give just some examples, we can improve memory through training, and that generally improves our performance on most things related to Intelligence even if it isn't intelligence <i>per-se</i> (and guess what, it is considered a part of intelligence by most RPGs, including WoD). We can improve many &quot;tangential&quot; skills like pattern recognition, stimuli discrimination and others that scientists use other words to describe, but for our discussion fall within the game's definition of Intelligence. We can learn new reasoning methods, specially through learning new languages, that increase our cognitive problem-solving tools.<br /> <br /> Does it matter if any of those things is technically labeled Intelligence in scientific papers for the purposes of increasing a game stat? Overall I think we're well served in this sense, at least as much as we are with increasing any other attribute.<br /> <br /> Oh, and one important effect of such training is that while it may not really improve &quot;real&quot; intelligence significantly, it does have an effect in fighting off things that reduce our effective intelligence in real life. Most of the time we're not operating at our peak, and we have a tendency for decay of course. &quot;Mental training&quot;, even some of the most bogus ones, is good at helping us to lag less behind our own potential and to keep it or even recover through time.<br /> <br /> Just to clarify, I won't give a source here because the question is actually far bigger in real science because of all those things that aren't real Intelligence for us but are just as good for the game. A research for scientific papers on improving intelligence alone will give a wide range of data running through the gamut of possible answers and really won't help without picking up everything and reading for the details, and then your actual interest may be in some of the many traits that have other labels within academy. Even a sample would take me more time copying and pasting sources than typing this, so I'll really accept the doubt this time if it comes to that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T22:41:23-07:00","id":1492953,"title":"","content":"1- it's difficult to maintain a global conspiracy if the people you embrace are not only -average- Joes but average Joes that don't put in a lot of effort to reach the baseline of competent. If people are willing to go to university or college or to get apprentiships in order to improve their lives why wouldn't a vampire, who no longer has a time issue as he/she potentially has immortaility ahead of him/her, and who would likely feel some social obligation to their sire for granting them an opportunity, train? <br /> <br /> 2- Even if you are very skilled (and I assume most embraces are because vampires sire often for willing vasals that will improve their power.) there's still going to be defecient areas in your knowledge and your abilities that you need to patch up. The scholarly vampire needs to know how to hunt and how to control things well when things need to get physical. The soldier needs peacetime skills so he doesn't outlive his usefulness/become a dependent drain on the sire's resources.<br /> <br /> 3- Although it's pretty established that vampires usually don't embrace the Best of the Best because they've got some level of fame, that doesn't mean they don't prefer to embrace accomplished people. Also I wouldn't trust city books to give you a -standard- because those are set in more dysfunctional cities designed to provide more conflict and stakes and drama. <br /> <br /> <br /> I'd expect really rapid development in many skills within the first few months of being a vampire, with skills like brawl, larceny, stealth, subterfuge, performance, politics, occult and law all shooting up from nothing to the second or third level. <i>all </i>vampires should learn competent brawl and subterfuge. Vampires in the Camarilla, Giovanni and FoS should all be learning politics, law and to a lesser extent finance (you can get a ghoul for that but having entry level knowledge to keep on top of things is really essential.) Vampires in the cults should be gaining whatever abilities their doctrine encourages: The FoS getting Occult and Crafts; The Assamites warriors getting Stealth, Melee, Survival, medicine; All good sabbat should know how to build explosives (and other stuff I'm sure, it's a big sect)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T22:57:43-07:00","id":1492956,"title":"","content":"Why would you want them to learn brawl as opposed to learning to use a gun or melee weapon? Learning performance also seems kind of weird for a must have. Whats useful about singing and dancing for a vampire? And why would they need to personally know law, are they planning on defending themselves in court without a lawyer?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Exalted Whose Name is Carved in Leaves of Jade<br /> Senator of the Greater Chamber<br /> Champion of the Silver Pact<br /> Chosen of the Little Gods<br /> Agent of Fate","edited":{"userid":988,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/988-mizu","user":"Mizu","date":". 09-13-2022, 02:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/988-mizu","name":"Mizu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-12T23:17:24-07:00","id":1492957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mizu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow?p=1492956#post1492956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why would you want them to learn brawl as opposed to learning to use a gun or melee weapon?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because it's overwhelmingly the better option for emergencies during night-to-night feeding? IDK about you but blasting someone and then sucking them down while they're bleeding to death on the floor is a rather suboptimal feeding strategy when it comes to attention and Humanity. Grappling, on the otherhand, is a great non-lethal method to subdue someone should there be any accident feeding. And if Assault is for some reason your go-to-feeding option, it's still a lot better on the masquerade and not drawing attention. You're not trying to kill people. you don't need to cause people excessive harm. As a vampire using ambush you're very likely going to win while unarmed anyway even if they pull something out on you, so you don't need the advantage of a weapon.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking getting a body count is a noob move, and so is using a weapon where it's not necessary. Using a weapon for intimidation's is in most cases actually better than using the weapon. You've certainly made a colossal mistake if you have to whip something out during a routine feed. Mastering wrestling is a great way to stop things from getting out of control. Once you're firmly in control and the subject is in your arms rather than bleeding to death, you have options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 09-13-2022, 02:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-13T08:16:45-07:00","id":1492974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1492896-how-fast-do-you-think-vampires-should-grow?p=1492953#post1492953\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1- it's difficult to maintain a global conspiracy if the people you embrace are not only -average- Joes but average Joes that don't put in a lot of effort to reach the baseline of competent.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, mediocrity is relative to context. And a lot of people that go to university are quite mediocre there.<br /> <br /> But I agree that the first nights would be a growth spur, that's only natural. It is when you have the most to learn. Probably the first 3 to 5 dots are the fastest learning in the entire life of most vampires.<br /> <br /> Yet I disagree on the rest. Even a global conspiracy doesn't need this level of competence, necessarily. Each only needs the bare minimum to accomplish their niches, and behind most mortal activities 3 Discipline dots and the innate vampire abilities is good enough. While I do agree that the average vampire will be more skilled than the average mortal, such an ideal spread as you suggests is quite excessive.<br /> <br /> I think most vampires will accomplish in the first few months to about a year to enhance their most common rolls to about 6 dice, give or take one. This is two dice above the average mortal. They'll have something like that for their up to three Discipline rolls and maybe one specialist roll with up to 8 dice, again 2 above the average established mortal professional. On most other skills avoiding the need to roll is far more effective than trying to learn everything. Networking among both vampires and mortals helps a lot with compensating your flaws, and then you just avoid having to roll bad pools. Like normal people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-13T13:45:52-07:00","id":1493023,"title":"","content":"Elysium which is the best oficial VTM book there is in my humble opinion had a table of XP per year on average. The interesting thing was that elders have more trouble learning and get less XP per year than for example a neonate. In case it helps I however do is this:<br /> <br /> <b>XP per sesion</b><ul><li>1 + (the average of intelligence + courage rounded down).</li> <li>Max amount of XP per session is six</li> </ul><br /> <b>Spending XP</b><br /> Experience can only be expended in leisure time. Depending of the LV of what you want to buy you need a minimun amount of time<br /> <br /> \ud83d\udd34 No time required<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 1 day<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 1 Week<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 1 month<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 6 Months<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34+ 1 year per point above 5.<br /> <br /> Note: Under exceptional circunstances like diablerie or the usage of blood magic to unlock potential this requisites may be ignored<br /> <br /> <b>Learning disciplines (Optional)</b><br /> <br /> As the beast sees feeding as a ritual in order to advance the disciplines not only is necesary practice, but also a proper sacrifice. Thus the vampire must kill a victim drainning all his life. The more a vampire wants to develop his powers the less human will he become which is one of the reasons why most vampires stop developing their powers. Special victims whose personality matches the discipline may provide a reduction in the XP cost.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":24037,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","user":"Leandro16","date":". 09-13-2022, 04:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-14T05:12:11-07:00","id":1493057,"title":"","content":"[QUOTE=Leandro16;n1493023]Elysium which is the best oficial VTM book there is in my humble opinion had a table of XP per year on average. The interesting thing was that elders have more trouble learning and get less XP per year than for example a neonate. In case it helps I however do is this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>XP per sesion</b><ul><li>1 + (the average of intelligence + courage rounded down).</li> <li>Max amount of XP per session is six</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div> Tremere master race, amirte?<br /> But more seriously, I don't think Inteligence has much to do with picking up skills or abilities IRL. If anything, Very brainy people are often lazy or overly absorbed in niche things *cough* while less absorbent people often compensate by working really hard or by better assessing their situation and getting the help they need. If there was a stat to dictate how fast people learn, i'd rather use willpower, resources, perception... It certainly wouldn't be intelligence. I think Intelligence is great when it comes to applying knowledge, and it's good for sorting bad information from good information, but the effects on acquisition and internalization aren't much. In my experience the biggest dictator of how well someone is going to learn is actually from how interested and engaged they actually are in the subject.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Spending XP</b><br /> I like your chart but really, I think -how long it would take to learn something- is pretty different. If you spend a week with a competent tutor learning X for several hours every day and can't walk away with your first dot because of an XP shortage, that's a shortcoming. While individual skills may differ immensely I'd say the rate might be like this for each dot:<br /> <br /> \ud83d\udd34 A couple of hours<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 a week<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 a month, assuming you push yourself<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 The better part of a year.<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34 talent dependent.<br /> \ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34\ud83d\udd34+ maybe a decade per dot over 5<br /> <br /> Therefore I think XP actually gets in the way of things and I'd rather do an XP-less game. (Or have the ST keep track of things and allocate fairly) because saying your character learns X during your downtime of more than a year and yet only having the XP to... not get any points at all... yeah that sucks. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Learning disciplines (Optional)</b><br /> <br /> As the beast sees feeding as a ritual in order to advance the disciplines not only is necesary practice, but also a proper sacrifice. Thus the vampire must kill a victim drainning all his life. The more a vampire wants to develop his powers the less human will he become which is one of the reasons why most vampires stop developing their powers. Special victims whose personality matches the discipline may provide a reduction in the XP cost. </div> </div> </div>This is too extreme. It's &quot;A Beast I am lest a Beast I become&quot; not &quot;I'm a fucking monster anyway you swing&quot;<br /> (But I would appreciate the delicious irony if this rule was applied to Bardo","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}